  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I read the dashboard and the rule back from Kibana afterwards to confirm them.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Flights\" (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 33 fields. Its time field is `timestamp` (a date). The main fields are:\n   - **Numbers:** `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `DistanceMiles`, `FlightDelayMin`, `FlightTimeMin`, `dayOfWeek`, `hour_of_day`.\n   - **Yes/no:** `Cancelled`, `FlightDelay`.\n   - **Text values:** `Carrier`, `FlightNum`, `FlightDelayType`, `FlightTimeHour`, plus origin and destination details (airport, city, country, region, weather).\n   - **Locations:** `OriginLocation`, `DestLocation`.\n   - The rest are Elasticsearch's built-in fields (`_id`, `_index` and similar).\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-7a214369 Flights\" (id `74c2be29-6aee-49c8-91eb-a0352e14f29c`) has exactly two bar-chart panels, both on that data view. One counts flights over `timestamp`; the other counts flights for the top 10 values of `Carrier`.\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-7a214369 Cancelled flights\" (id `26d8ae78-7e8e-4aa8-a37f-029d18f6a92e`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on the flights data view. It runs every minute, filters on `Cancelled : true`, and fires when there are more than 10 matching flights in the last 5 minutes. It was created enabled, so it didn't need enabling, and its first run finished with status `ok`.\n   - One setting you may want to change: I left on \"exclude hits from previous run\", which is Kibana's default. Because the rule runs every minute, each run may not count flights that the previous run already saw. If you want a straight count of everything in the 5-minute window, turn this off.\n4. **Edit and check:** the carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\", and the other panel is unchanged. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
